% ============================================================================
% celestial-objects — the basic types of celestial object and one short
% defining property its source states for each (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% One of the very first sorting tasks in astronomy, and a rung a SPACE-SCIENCE
% agent reasons UP from: the night sky is not one undifferentiated glitter, it
% is a handful of KINDS of object, and each kind has a characteristic property
% that TELLS IT APART from the others. "A star gives off its own light; a
% planet revolves around a star; a moon orbits a planet; a comet is frozen
% gases and dust; an asteroid is rocky." Before you can reason about a point of
% light — is it shining or reflecting? orbiting the Sun or a planet? — you must
% first know WHICH kind of celestial object you are looking at. Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? celestial_property(star, $Property)      % gives_off_light
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `celestial_property(object, property)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the property WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these basic celestial-object types (it
% never invents a property).
%
% Each basic type of celestial object and the short defining property the
% source states for it, as a little truth table:
%
%     celestial object   defining property (source's word)   a beginner's cue
%     ----------------   ---------------------------------   ------------------
%     star               gives_off_light                     it shines by itself
%     planet             revolves_around_star                it goes around a star
%     moon               orbits_planet                       it goes around a planet
%     comet              frozen_gases_and_dust               a dirty cosmic snowball
%     asteroid           rocky                               a rocky leftover chunk
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a property and recall which object it
% defines:
%
%     ? celestial_property($Object, orbits_planet)   % moon
%
% A word that is NOT one of these basic celestial-object types — `cloud`,
% `galaxy`, `nebula` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it
% rather than inventing a property. That honest-abstention property is what
% makes the table safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the
% object→property pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `property` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to be a
% defining property the source EXPLICITLY states for that object — never one
% the source does not support. Where the everyday word differs from the
% source's word — the source says a star "gives off ... light" (not "emits"),
% a planet "revolves around a star" (not "orbits the Sun") — the atom uses the
% source's own word, so every value stays literally checkable against the
% quoted span.
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every object→property pair
% below was read out of a fetched NASA page, and any pair whose property could
% not be verified there would have been dropped). Each pair, with the primary
% source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   star → gives_off_light
%     https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/universe_level1/stars.html
%     (NASA StarChild — NASA Goddard Space Flight Center)
%     "A star is a big ball of gas which gives off both heat and light."
%
%   planet → revolves_around_star
%     https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/solar_system_level1/planets.html
%     (NASA StarChild — NASA Goddard Space Flight Center)
%     "A planet is a large space object which revolves around a star."
%
%   moon → orbits_planet
%     https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/moons/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Naturally-formed bodies that orbit planets are called moons, or
%      planetary satellites."
%
%   comet → frozen_gases_and_dust
%     https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Comets are cosmic snowballs of frozen gases, rock, and dust that orbit
%      the Sun."
%
%   asteroid → rocky
%     https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/
%     (NASA Science — U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
%     "Asteroids, sometimes called minor planets, are rocky, airless remnants
%      left over from the early formation of our solar system about 4.6 billion
%      years ago."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NASA StarChild statement
% that fixes the first row (star → gives_off_light). Every OTHER row's per-fact
% source and verbatim span is listed above, so each property remains
% independently checkable. All sources are NASA (StarChild / NASA Science), a
% primary U.S. government (National Aeronautics and Space Administration)
% resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table celestial_property {
    columns object, property

    row (star, gives_off_light)
    row (planet, revolves_around_star)
    row (moon, orbits_planet)
    row (comet, frozen_gases_and_dust)
    row (asteroid, rocky)

    source "A star is a big ball of gas which gives off both heat and light."
    locator "https://starchild.gsfc.nasa.gov/docs/StarChild/universe_level1/stars.html"
    trust authoritative
}
